think chose OpenCode over the field researched earlier this session (dsh, Cline, Fireworks-tooling, Aider) on the same grounds the day's own harness research already named it: most-used in the world by that research's own count, and Together AI is a first-class named provider in its own config schema rather than a bolt-on
think checked nixpkgs for opencode before reaching for the curl-pipe-to-bash installer its own docs lead with, per this pier's own declared-host-config.md discipline -- found it packaged at 1.15.10, confirmed genuinely fetchable by curling its real /nix/store hash's narinfo against cache.nixos.org (HTTP 200), and confirmed it actually runs before declaring it anywhere
think searched upstream's own release history to check the version gap honestly rather than assuming nixpkgs tracked current -- found v2.0.11 tagged the same day as this session, one major version ahead of nixpkgs' 1.15.10, and named the gap in both the configuration.nix comment and open/HARNESS_SETUP.md rather than silently carrying a stale claim of currency
think tried the documented /connect TUI flow first and found it does not accept piped stdin cleanly -- the fuzzy-finder reads raw keystrokes and a newline sent through a pipe does not register as Enter the way an interactive terminal would, so the search field kept accumulating characters rather than confirming a selection
think switched to writing the two config files directly rather than fighting the TUI -- searched for and confirmed OpenCode's own documented auth.json schema ({ type: "api", key: string }) from its own GitHub-sourced documentation before writing anything, rather than guessing a plausible-looking shape
think proved the whole chain with one real call rather than trusting each layer in isolation: `opencode run` against `together/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813` answered the exact requested string, `opencode-together-live`, confirming harness, provider config, credential file, and model all connected correctly in one pass
think added opencode to nixos/configuration.nix's systemPackages and made the EXACT SAME syntax mistake this same room caught in the prior nixos round -- a bare package name written as a top-level statement outside the systemPackages list rather than inside it -- caught it again before running any evaluation, by re-reading the diff rather than trusting that the edit looked similar enough to the working huggingface-hub/llm lines above it
think re-ran the same two-step proof the prior nixos round established: `nix eval` over the whole systemPackages list confirmed opencode-1.15.10 parses inside the real module, then `nix build '.#nixosConfigurations.pier.config.system.build.toplevel' --no-link` built the entire system closure clean, no sudo, nothing touched on the running host
think looked for real datacenter evidence rather than restating Together's own policy a second time -- inspected the full response headers of a live call with curl -D - and found x-request-id carrying a shape like <uuid>-aws_ue2, which repeats inside the JSON body's own id field; ran three more live calls (two more DeepSeek, one GLM-5.3) and all three landed aws_ue2, with the very first auth-test call from an earlier round already on record as aws_ue1 -- both AWS US regions
think named the limit of that evidence honestly in both open/HARNESS_SETUP.md and open/US_DATACENTER_POLICY.md rather than overclaiming it: four samples describe four samples, not a guarantee, and this measures the stated default's real behavior rather than replacing the need for a formal audited SLA should one ever be required
think fetched Together's own live /v1/models catalog with the real working key to find GLM-5.3's exact slug (zai-org/GLM-5.3) rather than guessing at a plausible spelling, and called it live to confirm it answers and to get a second, independent aws_ue2 reading beside DeepSeek's
think confirmed Aider (aider-chat, nixpkgs version 0.86.1) as the named choice per the operator's own ask for options, checking that it is LiteLLM-backed and reads the same TOGETHER_API_KEY environment variable already set on this pier, rather than requiring a second, differently-named credential
think wrote open/HARNESS_SETUP.md as its own new page rather than folding it into PROVIDER_SETUP.md, since it answers a different question again -- not which provider, but which harness sits on top of the chosen provider -- matching the same reasoning that split US_DATACENTER_POLICY.md and PROVIDER_COMPARISON.md out as their own pages earlier this same room
think updated US_DATACENTER_POLICY.md's Together AI section in place, adding the measured finding directly beside the policy claim it strengthens rather than appending an unrelated addendum, and cross-linked to HARNESS_SETUP.md's own Part 6 for the full method and the re-runnable command
think ran the whole-tree register scan, the ASCII scan, and the link lint after every file change this round, per the by-now-established habit -- every check read ok or clean on the first pass
obs the auth.json write happened once, from this session, using the same env var this session already held from the earlier live Together auth test -- the real key was never re-typed or re-pasted anywhere this round, and a fresh grep across the whole tree for the key's own substring came back empty before this log was written
obs the caught nixos syntax error is now the second time this exact class of mistake has surfaced in this room -- a package name that looks like it belongs at the top level of a module because a nearby comment block sits at that same indentation -- worth naming plainly rather than treating as a one-off, since a third occurrence would mean the habit of re-reading the diff before evaluating had not actually formed
